Falsifies `machine_discovery::x86_64::verification::no_length_of_handoff_bytes_makes_the_decode_read_past_its_end`.

The defect: the length check for the version-1 structure is gone, so the memory-map
fields at offsets 40 and 48 are read out of any handoff whose `version` word says 1
or more, including one that is only 40 bytes long.

The two facts that have to line up are written by different people: the *length* of
the buffer is whatever the kernel mapped, and the *version* is a `u32` the loader
wrote inside it. So the guard is on one number and the offsets it protects are on
another, four lines apart, and a loader that writes a version-1 header into a
version-0 allocation is a boot-protocol bug this parser is supposed to survive
rather than dereference.

`BootInfo::parse` is the first thing the x86 kernel does with a pointer it did not
choose. The module's own tests supply exactly 40 bytes and exactly 56 bytes; every
length between the two is the gap this defect lives in.

Expected to fail: the bounds check inside `u64(bytes, 40)`.

